<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Sharpe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Sharpe was an enslaved Baptist deacon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> who planned and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Christmas Rebellion</a> of 1831–1832, known as the Baptist War. He intended a peaceful general strike for wages, which turned into a widespread armed revolt across western Jamaica. He was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> in May 1832 and is honoured as a National Hero of Jamaica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe.jpg" alt="Samuel Sharpe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: english wikipedia user Pozole · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsharpe.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sharpe was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the parish of St James, on a plantation owned by Samuel and Batty Sharpe. The Slave Return of 1832 that recorded his death named him Archer, also called Samuel Sharpe, the son of Eve, and a return of 1817 lists Archer on the plantation with his mother Juda Bligom and his siblings Joe and Eliza. He was allowed an education, which earned him respect among the enslaved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became a preacher and a deacon of the Burchell Baptist Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a>, whose pastor was Thomas Burchell, a missionary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and travelled between parishes teaching the enslaved a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that he believed promised freedom. In the mistaken belief that emancipation had already been granted, he organised a strike across many estates during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> harvest, when the planters most needed labour.</p><p>The rebellion began on 27 December 1831 at the Kensington Estate. After reprisals by the planters the rebels burned crops, and the protest became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s largest slave rebellion, mobilising as many as 60,000 enslaved people. Colonial troops and Maroon warriors suppressed it within two weeks. Some 14 whites were killed, while troops killed more than 200 of the enslaved, and between 310 and 340 people were executed after trials, many for property offences such as stealing livestock.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In jail Sharpe met the missionary Henry Bleby, who reported him saying: &quot;I would rather die upon yonder gallows than live my life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.&quot; The rebellion and its repression led to two parliamentary inquiries and are believed to have helped bring about the Slavery Abolition Act 1833 and the end of slavery across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in 1838. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> proclaimed him a National Hero on 31 March 1982, with the posthumous title Rt. Excellent Samuel Sharpe.</p><p>Sam Sharpe Teachers' College in Granville, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a>, was founded in his honour in 1975. His portrait appeared on the Jamaican $50 note and, from 2023, on the new $500 note. The saxophonist Courtney Pine recorded a tribute, Samuel Sharpe, on his 2012 album House of Legends.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Sharpe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the British Caribbean the enslaved rose in Barbados in 1816 and Demerara in 1823, and at Christmas 1831 tens of thousands in western Jamaica followed the Baptist deacon <b>Samuel Sharpe</b> in a strike that became the Baptist War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samuel Sharpe</b>, an enslaved Baptist deacon, intended a peaceful general strike for wages; it turned into an armed revolt of up to 60,000 enslaved people in which many plantations were burned.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance was constant, from Tacky's Revolt of 1760 to the Baptist War of 1831 to 1832 led by <b>Samuel Sharpe</b>, which hastened emancipation in 1834 to 1838.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-jamaica">Colony of Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.47,-77.92&amp;year=1821" aria-label="Montego Bay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2439 1479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Sharpe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2889 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2889 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.47,-77.92&amp;year=1821">What was here in 1821? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
